course: dictionary
lesson: 897
title: Bison: an animal that nearly died out
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bison is a large wild animal of the cattle family. It has a big shaggy head, curved horns and a hump on its back. When the text says «a herd of bison», picture a big group of them moving together.
explain: There are two kinds. American bison live in North America and are often called buffalo. European bison, also called wisent, live in forests in Europe. Look at the words around the bison: a place name tells you which kind is meant.
explain: The sentences tell a story. «European bison were wiped out in the wild by 1927, but about 50 lived in zoos.» Wiped out means every wild one was gone. Words like bred and released help you follow what happened next.
fact: A bison is a large wild animal of the cattle family.
fact: Bison can be one bison or many bison, and a group of them is a herd.
fact: American bison are often called buffalo.
fact: European bison, or wisent, live in forests in Europe.
fact: «Wiped out» means killed off completely, so none are left in the wild.
exercise: «A herd of bison crossed the plain.» What is a herd? | *A group of animals moving together | A lake | A single wild cow | A kind of tent
exercise: «European bison were wiped out in the wild by 1927.» What does wiped out mean? | *All gone, killed off completely | Fed and made bigger | Painted with mud | Moved to a new farm
exercise: Which kind of bison is often called buffalo? | European bison | *American bison | Wisent | Bison in zoos
test: «Native American peoples used bison for food, clothes and tools.» What did they use bison for? | Pulling ships | Sport and games | Building stone towers only | *Food, clothes and tools
test: Where do European bison live? | In big cities | In North American deserts | *In forests in Europe | On islands in the sea
exam: Which description best fits a bison? | A kind of fish that lives in rivers | A tall tree in a forest | *A large wild animal of the cattle family with a hump on its back | A small pink farm pig
